    "Text": "1. ARCHETYPE AND SYMBOLISM\nThe Three of Cups is a card of diversity in love, temptation and duality. It represents indecision, but also the desire for constant movement, exploration of life and emotional freedom.\nCard number: 3\n\n2. STRENGTHS\nPeople with this birth card are extremely curious, with excellent mental abilities and a talent for analysis. They are charming, communicative and progressive in their work. They have strong creative and artistic gifts, and their main advantage is the ability to express themselves. They are ambitious and can achieve success in almost anything, especially if they travel or generate new ideas.\n\n3. CHALLENGES\nThe main problem is indecision and fear of commitment, which lead to emotional conflicts. They often experience anxiety about money and fear of poverty, which prevents them from focusing and finishing what they have started. They are prone to mental and emotional delusions, insecurity in feelings and even lying in relationships in order to maintain variety or avoid confrontation.\n\n4. LOVE AND RELATIONSHIPS\nIn love, the Three of Cups is fickle and seeks variety, often falling into a \"carousel\" of relationships that brings emotional exhaustion. They experience uncertainty about whether they are loved and are prone to experiments, including in the sexual sphere. They like intelligent partners. Men are attracted to women of the suit of Spathia and Diamond. Women have complex relationships with Spathia and Cup men, and Spade men find them attractive, but unsuitable for a family.\n\n5. WORK AND MONEY\nTo be successful, they need to be on the move - to travel or often change something in their daily lives. They are suitable for creative professions and fields that require communication and ideas. They can improve their financial situation through innovative ideas or travel, but they must overcome the fear of poverty to achieve stability.\n\n6. KARMIC LESSON\nThe two most important lessons are: to overcome the fear of poverty, believing that they will always find the means when necessary, and to transform their indecision into creativity. They must learn to love themselves, so as not to desperately seek validation from others, and to understand that stability and trust are more valuable than constant variety.\n\n7. LIFE CYCLES\nMercury (0-13 years): Accumulation of knowledge, avid reading of everything they find interesting.\nVenus (13-26 years): Continued accumulation of knowledge, development of intuition, emergence of a desire for parenthood.\nMars (26-39 years): Hard and successful work in the chosen field of activity.\nJupiter (39-52): Changing worldview and life philosophy.\nSaturn (52+): Working in several directions and improving well-being."
